# Nuclear level density by exact many-fermion state counting.
#
# The number of N-fermion states at total single-particle energy E is the
# coefficient  [z^N q^E]  of  prod_k (1 + z q^{e_k})  over single-particle
# levels e_k. We build it with a fixed-particle-number knapsack DP:
#     A[n][e] = number of ways to place n particles with total s.p. energy e.
# The level density rho(E_exc) reads off the row n = N at energy Egs + E_exc.
#
# For EQUALLY SPACED single-particle levels this is provably the integer
# partition function p(E_exc)  (1,1,2,3,5,7,11,...), which asymptotes to the
# Fermi-gas / Bethe form  rho ~ exp(2 sqrt(a E)).  That exact identity is the
# validation. Pure integer/float array DP -- no eigensolver -- so it compiles
# straight through the C backend to wasm.

N = 40                 # number of fermions (fills the lowest N levels)
M = 120                # number of single-particle levels available
Emax = 40              # highest excitation energy to report
Egs = 780              # ground-state s.p. energy = N(N-1)/2 = 0+1+...+39
Etot = Egs + Emax
W = Etot + 1

# equally spaced single-particle levels 0, 1, 2, ..., M-1
lev = zeros(M)
for k to M { lev[k] = k * 1.0 }

# A[n*W + e] = number of ways to place n particles with total s.p. energy e
A = zeros((N + 1) * W)
A[0] = 1.0

for k to M {
    ek = lev[k]
    for i to N {
        nn = N - i                                  # descend n (0/1 knapsack)
        for e to W {
            if e >= ek {
                A[nn * W + e] = A[nn * W + e] + A[(nn - 1) * W + (e - ek)]
            }
        }
    }
}

# level density vs excitation energy
rho = zeros(Emax + 1)
for m to (Emax + 1) { rho[m] = A[N * W + (Egs + m)] }
show rho

